# Coordination polymer gels with important environmental and biological applications

## abstract
Coordination Polymer Gels (CPGs) constitute a subset of solid-like metal ion and bridging organic ligand structures (similar to metal-organic frameworks) that form multi-dimensional networks through a trapped solvent as a result of non-covalent interactions. While physical properties of these gels are similar to conventional high molecular weight organic polymer gels, coordination polymer gel systems are often fully reversible and can be assembled and disassembled in the presence of additional energy (heat, sonication, shaking) to give a solution of solvated gelators. Compared to gels resulting from purely organic self-assembled low molecular weight gelators, metal ions incorporated into the fibrilar networks spanning the bulk solvent can impart CPGs with added functionalities. The solid/liquid nature of the gels allows for species to migrate through the gel system and interact with metals, ligands, and the solvent. Chemosensing, catalysis, fluorescence, and drug-delivery applications are some of the many potential uses for these dynamic systems, taking advantage of the metal ion's coordination, the organic polydentate ligand's orientation and functionality, or a combination of these properties. By fine tuning these systems through metal ion and ligand selection and by directing self-assembly with external stimuli the rational synthesis of practical systems can be envisaged.

Coordination Polymer Gels (CPGs) constitute a subset of solid-like metal ion and bridging organic ligand structures (similar to metal–organic frameworks) that form multi-dimensional networks through a trapped solvent as a result of non-covalent interactions. While physical properties of these gels are similar to conventional high molecular weight organic polymer gels, coordination polymer gel systems are often fully reversible and can be assembled and disassembled in the presence of additional energy (heat, sonication, shaking) to give a solution of solvated gelators. Compared to gels resulting from purely organic self-assembled low molecular weight gelators, metal ions incorporated into the fibrilar networks spanning the bulk solvent can impart CPGs with added functionalities. The solid/liquid nature of the gels allows for species to migrate through the gel system and interact with metals, ligands, and the solvent. Chemosensing, catalysis, fluorescence, and drug-delivery applications are some of the many potential uses for these dynamic systems, taking advantage of the metal ion’s coordination, the organic polydentate ligand’s orientation and functionality, or a combination of these properties. By fine tuning these systems through metal ion and ligand selection and by directing self-assembly with external stimuli the rational synthesis of practical systems can be envisaged.
## Key learning points
1. Methods for preparation of coordination polymer gels.   
2. Physical properties that can be obtained with coordination polymer gels.
3. Applications of coordination polymer gels in biological and environmental fields. 4. Supramolecular structures to form coordination polymer gels.
## 1. Introduction
Gels are easily recognized as soft materials with a wide range of applications in cosmetics, pharmaceuticals, and in the food industry.1 Gelled materials are colloidal systems comprising two coexisting phases; a liquid-like phase amongst a solid network, the latter preventing the bulk flow of the liquid.2 Recently, increasing focus has been directed towards understanding the self-assembly and subsequent behaviour of these materials.3 The results from these studies are used to design carefully tailored gel materials for practical applications from tissue engineering4 to nanoscale electronics.5
In the late nineteenth century scientists observed that certain natural products were capable of thickening liquid samples, and in the twentieth century electron microscopy began to probe the nanostructures of these gels.6 The study of gels constitutes an early example of the ‘bottom-up’ approach to self-assembly, as microscopy reveals that molecular building blocks assemble into easily visualized fibrillar ‘solid-like’ networks which span the ‘liquid-like’ phase.7
Low molecular-weight organogelators are molecules capable of forming thermoreversible physical gels at concentrations typically lower than $5\%$ weight gelator/weight solvent.8–11 During gelation highly anisotropic 3D structures often form into fibres, ribbons, platelets, tubular structures or cylinders.10 Organogelators fall into two categories according to the force driving their molecular aggregation: hydrogen-bond based gelators9 and non-hydrogen-bond based gelators11 (Fig. 1). Typical examples of the former include aliphatic amide12 or urea-coupled cyclohexane,13 peptide,14 and sugar15 based derivatives that exhibit distinctive helical structures.16 Nonhydrogen-bond based gelators include cholesterol derivatives which can aggregate due to macrocyclic crown moieties, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, van der Waals forces and/or solvophobic properties.17 Any gel containing metal ions and bridging ligands may be classified as a metallogel, with coordination polymer gels (CPGs) as a subset in this category, and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) as a subset within CPGs (Fig. 1).18 Hence, CPGs may be considered metallogels under this broad definition. Metallogels, CPGs and MOFs all form by means of intermolecular interactions such as $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, hydrogen-bonds and hydrophobic interaction, while CPGs possess an extended structure of metal ions and bridging ligands which adopt 1D, 2D or 3D structures, while 2D and 3D structures exhibiting long-range order are often classified as MOFs.19 Systems in which fibers or helices are comprised solely of organic species, with linkages between these strands comprising metal ion centres, are also labeled as CPs (roughly analogous to block copolymers).19
Kimizuka and co-workers provided an excellent example of a CPG reporting the first example of thermally reversible, heat-set gel networks in organic media.20 A lipophilic ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ complex of 4-(3-lauryloxy)propyl-1,2.4-triazole (1) can be dissolved in chloroform to afford a blue gel at room temperature. Upon cooling below $25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ the gel turns into a pale pink solution resulting from a fully reversible thermochromic transition from tetrahedral ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ geometry to octahedral (Fig. 2).21
Recently, CPGs have exploited for useful functional properties such as physical stimuli response, redox responsiveness, catalysis, phosphorescent behaviour, and spin-crossover phenomenon.22 Furthermore, by selecting the binding metal ions and gelator molecules one can affect self-assembly modes and finely tune gelation conditions. Much like in supramolecular chemistry, chemists have begun to rationally design CPG ligands and gelators with suitable coordination geometries for the incorporation of specific metals to generate scaffolds containing void spaces. Cavities amongst the
