This review focuses on the applications of gel forming functional ligands and metal ions in sensing, drug-delivery and catalysis that have been developed over the past three to five years. Selected ligands include pyridine, tetrazole, carboxylic acid and amino acid moieties which bind to metal ions to form coordination polymer gels in organic solvents or water, and are characterized by their non-discrete structures which self-assemble to form fibrillar networks. The structure of the coordination polymer network is strongly dependent on the metal ions. In addition, these coordination polymer gels can selectively detect and separate specific guest molecules in environmental and biological samples. We describe coordination polymer gels that are useful in biological and environmental fields that have been developed in our research laboratory and by other groups.
## 2. Catalytic calix[4]arene-based gels
Calixarenes are one of the macrocyclic receptors known to date alongside crown ethers, cyclodextrins, cryptands, and cucurbiturils.22,23 These are synthesized from the base-catalyzed condensation reaction of $p$ -tert-butyl phenol and formaldehyde.23 Calixarenes systems possess several advantageous properties including easy organic modification at both the lower and upper rims, and they can be adorned by a structured, yet flexible binding core. Additionally calixarenes may possess a hydrophobic cavity imparting the structure with simultaneous hydrophobic and hydrophilic properties.23 A calix[4]arene, with four binding sites, will promote cross-linking to form the polymeric network leading to gelation; the cavity of a calix[4]arene provides additional interstitial space to ‘hold’ the solvent molecules.
Xu et al.24 reported the use of the calix[4]arene derivative (2) as a ligand to form a coordination polymer gel. Choosing $\big[\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{en})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}\big]^{2+}$ ( $\mathrm{{en}=}$ ethylenediamine) as the metal complex precursor, polymerization was monitored by ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR in DMSO- ${\bf\ddot{d}}_{6}$ . Immediately after adding $\mathrm{\big[Pd(en)(H_{2}O)_{2}\big](N O_{3})_{2}}$ to the DMSO solution of 2, the peaks of the aromatic protons broadened and the broad peaks of the methylene proton at $\delta\ =\ 4.30$ and 3.65 disappeared. Nevertheless, the peaks of protons on the ethylenediamine shifted and broadened, indicating coordination based polymerization. These peaks became broader and less intense after the gelation was confirmed as complete by the inverted test-tube method (Fig. 3). Considering the conformational flexibility of 2 demonstrated by ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR, DMSO fills the interstitial space of random conformers of 2 cross-linked by the $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{en})^{2+}$ ions. The typical molar ratios of $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ to 2 were from $1.8:1$ to $2:1$ , and the percentages of the coordination polymers in the gels range from 0.5 to $2\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ ( $\cdot\mathrm{\sim}1\ \mathrm{mmol}$ concentrations). Changing the coordination complex gel precursor alters the nature of gelation, for example substituting ethylenediamine with a 1,5-cyclooctadiene (COD) ligand. Using $\mathbf{Pd}(\mathbf{COD})\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ as the metal complex precursor, gelation was observed in DMSO after $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ , which was longer than that of $\mathrm{\big[Pd(en)(H_{2}O)_{2}\big](N O_{3})_{2}}$ (typically 4 h)
