To measure solvent diffusion through the gel network, toluene was employed as a non-ionic organic substrate due to its observable UV absorbance allowing for easy monitoring at low concentration. A freshly made aqueous solution of toluene $\left(570\ \mathrm{ppm}\right)$ was added to $0.4~\mathrm{ml}$ of the gel. The gel’s toluene concentration was measured by timed acquisition of UV absorbance spectra of the aqueous solution by monitoring the decrease in the absorbance until it became undetectable. The ‘uptake’ follows first-order kinetics suggesting and that it is most likely analogous to physical adsorption observed in nature. The partition coefficient, $K,$ was used to define the ability for the ‘uptake’ of solvent by the coordination polymer gel. The final concentration of toluene in the gel was found to be $18350\ \mathrm{ppm}$ when the final concentration of toluene in water is $386~\mathrm{ppm}$ . The calculated value of $K$ is $\sim47.0\pm2.0$ , which suggests that $\Delta G_{298}=$ $22.3\pm0.4\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-1}$ for the ‘uptake’ of toluene from water into the coordination polymer gel at room temperature. The same experiment was run with chlorobenzene and $K$ was calculated to be $36.0\pm$ 2.0 $(\Delta G_{298}=22.1\pm0.4\mathrm{{kcal}\ m o l^{-1})}$ .
Additionally, Xu et al.24 also investigated the catalytic properties of pyridine-based calix[4]arene (2) coordination polymer gels (Table 1). Adding the palladium complex 7 into a solution of ligand 2 in DMSO, a coordination polymer gel 2 formed in six hours. The cone shape and the conformational flexibility of calix[4]arene (2) may facilitate solvent trapping and contribute to the higher solvent content than in the corresponding coordination polymer gels. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) shows fibrous structures with diameters smaller than the wavelength of visible light of $\sim70\pm120\ \mathrm{nm}$ in the coordination polymer gel 2, consistent with their transparency. The fiber in the coordination polymer gel displays open cellular structures, a typical observation with gels.
The catalytic ability of the pyridine-based calix[4]arene gel 2 was then evaluated for the oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde (Fig. 4). Pure benzyl alcohol was added to a freshly prepared CPG (molar ratio of catalyst and substrate is about $1:10^{4}\$ , and molecular oxygen was introduced by bubbling air into the reaction mixture. By monitoring the conversion of benzyl alcohol into benzaldehyde by HPLC every ten minutes over two hours it was noted that catalysis did not affect the stability of the coordination polymer gel. Dried gels with an equal amount of catalytic metal sites were tested under the same reaction conditions. The catalytic turnovers of all the gels were calculated and compared with the catalytic turnovers of 6 or 7 under similar reaction conditions. Fig. 4 shows the turnovers of different catalysts after two hours of reaction. For the reactions involving $[\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}]$ moieties, the turnovers follow the order: $\mathbf{B}>\mathbf{A}>\daleth>\mathrm{d}\mathbf{y}\mathbf{A}$ $>$ dry B. The catalytic turnover of A is more than twice that of the corresponding dry gel and greater than 1.5 times that of 7; the catalytic turnover of B is twice that of $[{\mathrm{Pd}}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}],$ three times that of the corresponding dry gel, and four times that of similar systems25 reported. Pyridine is not included in the oxidation reaction25 as these moieties are part of the polymer network that forms the gel thus eliminating the purification step of separating pyridine from benzaldehyde.25 These coordination polymer gels are stable in water and most organic solvents except acetone, and can catalyze the oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde, using their $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ moieties as catalytic centres with air as the oxygen source. The gelbased $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ catalysts do not form Pd black; the best catalytic turnover of the CPG is twice that of $[{\mathrm{Pd}}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}].$ .
## 3. Chiral coordination polymer gels
It has long been known that chirality plays a central role in controlling molecular recognition and interactions in living systems.26 Developing and discovering chirality mediated supramolecular assemblies are undoubtedly significant for obtaining novel functional materials such as chiral sensing platforms. Although the impact of chirality on self-assembled systems including fibrillar networks and even supramolecular gels has been found to be profound, to our knowledge the formation of supramolecular metallogels finely controlled by ligand chirality has been hardly achieved so far. Coordination polymer gel formation has indeed been found to be generally related to the chirality of gelators, yet it is difficult to fully understand the effect of the gelator stereostructure on supramolecular gel formation and properties at present. Therefore, it remains a challenging subject that deserves to be explored.
For instance, Ogden et al.27 investigated the gelation mechanism and chiral coordination of a proline-functionalized calix[4]arene (8) complexed with ${\mathrm{La}}^{3+}$ . Atomic force microscopy of the wet gels on mica reveals that the fibrous structure of the gels was significantly altered in the racemate, consistent with changes observed in the bulk properties. The racemate appears to form linked spheroidal structures, whereas the pure chiral systems form more elongated fibrous structures. The less stable racemic gels were found to collapse and crystallize over a period of days, where the enantiomerically pure systems remained stable for months. The crystals that formed in the racemate’s case were of sufficient quality to carry out X-ray structure determination. The X-ray structure shows intermolecular inclusion of a proline moiety into the cavity of a neighbouring calixarene resulting in the formation of a helical structure (Fig. 5). Each helix is formed by a single enantiomer, either the D- or L-isomer, indicating that the individual enantiomers could form comparable structures, forming the fundamental unit to create larger fibres, and fibre bundles.
The lanthanum cation coordinates two supramolecular helices to form a 2D coordination polymer. The La atom bonds to both of proline’s carboxylate oxygen atoms and also to both of the atoms in the centrosymmetrically related molecule. The La atom is also bonded to the carboxylate oxygen O141 of the molecule related by a cell translation along the $b$ direction. The polymeric sheets are packed in the unit cell with the proline group (8) of the sheet inserted into the calixarene cavity related by a 2-fold screw axis. A key feature is that the La ions bridge two helices, each of which are formed by one of the isomers, i.e. the La bridges a helix of D-8 with a helix of $\mathbf{L}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{8}}$ .
## 4. pH responsive fluorescent zinc-muala hydrogels
Vittal et al.28 have reported the formation of a CPG of $N-\left(7-$ hydroxyl-4-methyl-8-coumarinyl)-glycine (9) with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ in the absence of long-chain hydrophobic groups. When a basic aqueous solution of 9 is reacted with $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ a hydrogel of $[\mathrm{Zn}({\bf9})(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\bf O})]_{n}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}{\bf O}$ (9) is formed instantly. The as-prepared Zn-9 hydrogel is $\mathsf{p H}$ responsive and converts to a clear colourless solution upon acidification to a $\mathsf{p H}$ of 2, but it is able to regain its gel structure when the $\mathsf{p H}$ is raised to 8. At acidic $\mathsf{p H}$ values, the ligand should be protonated, disrupting the complexation and breaking up the gel structure. Slight modification of the ligand greatly changes the system, as when aqueous solutions of 10 and $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ are mixed a viscous liquid is formed that flows upon inversion at room temperature, but which yields single crystals of $\mathrm{\small{[Zn(muala)(H_{2}O)]}}$  $0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (10).
It was demonstrated that in the absence of a suitable single crystal of gel 9, those from 10 can be used for X-ray crystallography (Fig. 6). The muala anion is coordinated to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ centre in a tridentate manner along with a water ligand and carboxylate oxygen from a neighboring unit forming a 1D coordination polymer. These 1D CPs zigzag in a criss-crossed fashion, such that the disk-like coumarin rings segregate to form hydrophobic pockets, assisted by various hydrogen bonds generated by the $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and $\mathbf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ donor groups and O atoms as hydrogen bond acceptors. Such packing can easily be expanded to form a hydrogel by accommodating the water molecules firmly in the interstitial hydrophilic pockets, together with hydrogen bond donors and acceptors.
The SEM image of freeze-dried 9 displayed a typical fibrillar network structure. The fibers are several micrometers long with diameters in the range $200{-}500~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . From the crystal structure of 10, it appears that the 1D coordination polymers aggregate to form fibrous nanostructures, which then further organize into a 3D network through noncovalent interactions to entrap water molecules. In particular the fluorescence of amino acid-based coordination polymer gels 9 and 10 was studied. A hydrogel of 9 exhibits a strong blue emission with a maximum at $440\mathrm{nm}$ and a pronounced enhancement in fluorescence compared to 9 in solution (Fig. 7a). A drastic decrease in fluorescence is observed when the pH of 9 is lowered. The dissociation of the complex leads to the disruption of the coordination polymeric gel structure quenching fluorescence. The strong fluorescence of 9 can be visualized by the naked eye under UV light (Fig. 7b and c). A fluorescence micrograph of freeze-dried 9 also displays a fibrous structure, with a strong blue emission (Fig. 7c).
## 5. Fluorescent tetrazole-based CP gels
The luminescence of the $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ coordination polymer gel with 11 was studied by time-resolved fluorescence confocal microscopy. The emission decay profiles were monitored at $\lambda$ $=405{-}490\ \mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ coordination polymer gel 11 (Fig. 9). The fluorescence decay of $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ coordination polymer gel 11 was fitted with a single exponential component yielding a lifetime of 3.78 ns, indicating that this emission is fluorescence. This result reflects the fact that the CP gel 11 in its aggregate state is more rigid, restricting the rotational and vibrational movements of molecules. The limited molecular motions decrease the nonradiative relaxation process, which leads to the fluorescence enhancement and longer lifetime.29
Jung et al.30 also reported a fluorescent coordination polymer gel with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ . The coordination polymer gel 12 with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ exhibited a strong blue emission with a maximum at $\lambda=$ $475\mathrm{nm}$ in DMF/MeOH.30 The fluorescence intensity is enhanced markedly as compared to sol 12 ${\mathit{\lambda}}_{\mathrm{hex}}=300~\mathrm{nm}{\mathit{\Omega}}$ . The photoluminescence of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ coordination polymer gel 12 can be seen by the naked eye under UV light. The absorption and emission properties of gel 12 with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ from DMF and DMA were similar to that obtained from DMF:MeOH. Conversely, solid ligand 12 without $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ did not show emission at any wavelength. Therefore, the strong emission of gel 12 with $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ originates from the self-assembled supramolecular structure formed through strong intermolecular forces and not from the monomeric ligand 12.
According to density functional theory (DFT) calculations, in the optimized structure of $\mathrm{[Zn({\bf12})(M e O H)(D M F)]^{2+}}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ is octahedrally coordinated with four tetrazole moieties of the ligand 12, in DMF and MeOH (Fig. 10). The distances between $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and the coordinated nitrogen atom of the ligand 12 were calculated to be in the range of $2.189-2.333\mathrm{~\AA~}$ while those between $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and the oxygen atom of DMF and MeOH were calculated to be 1.985 and 2.106 Å. In contrast to a previous report,20 two bromine atoms are replaced with two tetrazole moieties of ligand 12. As the ligand 12 is replaced, the intermolecular H-bonds are generated between four tetrazole moieties of the ligand 12.
## 6. Ditopic pyridyl-diketone-based CPG
Very recently, Lindoy et $a l.^{31}$ investigated ditopic pyridyl-betadiketone derivative complexes with a $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ metal counter ion. Among them only the meta-substituted diketone derivative (13) formed a CPG with $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ . Their group investigated the relationship between the gelation and single crystal structure; the single crystal of the polymeric $[\operatorname{Cu}(\mathbf{1}3)_{2}]_{n}$ is characterized by a typical square planar coordination of the beta-diketonato units (Fig. 11). It was found that in complex 13 with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , additional weak hydrogen bonds of the uncoordinated pyridine nitrogen and multi $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions of the aromatic fragments led to packed extended columnar arrays. The observed diverse weak binding pattern differs markedly from those in the two other complexes ( $\scriptstyle{\dot{o}}-$ and $p$ -substituent) and is manifested by very different solidstate structures. It is likely that this difference in the ligand series can be attributed to the changing position of the pyridine nitrogen atom in the pyridyl substituents, leading to the different structural arrangements and, in particular, differences in the weak intermolecular interactions linking complex units (Fig. 11).
## 7. CP gels as smart materials chemosensors
As the liquid-like solvent within a gel network is bound by weaker intermolecular forces, scientists have begun to exploit the fluidity of the trapped solvents and its ability to diffuse species through the fibrillar network. Chemosensing applications are of particular interest as gel networks and gel functionalized systems can respond to the introduction of species altering the conformation of the incorporated coordination compounds providing specific information regarding solvent contaminant. The development of chemosensing gels may provide useful qualitative and quantitative measures for the detection and potential isolation of toxic gases, or dangerous chemicals.
Jung et al.32 reported on a CPG with ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ and its application as a chemosensor for gases containing chlorine atoms. Ligand 12 immediately formed a gel upon addition of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ with a variety of counter anions such as $\mathrm{ClO_{4}}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{\mathrm{OAc}^{-}}$ , $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ , I, and ${\mathrm{NO_{3}}}^{-}$ in polar solvents such as DMF, DMA, and DMF/methanol $(1:1~\nu/\nu)$ when two solutions were mixed. Except for the solution containing $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , these gels exhibited a red color (Fig. 12). The coordination polymer gel 12 with $\mathbf{CoBr}_{2}$ shows a spherical structure with $20{-}30~\mathrm{~nm}$ diameter particles. The sample uniformity and narrow diameter distribution were confirmed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) as shown in Fig. 12C. In addition, TEM images of the coordination polymer gels obtained using different anions show a spherical structure with $20{-}30~\mathrm{nm}$ diameter particles.
The coordination structure was optimized for ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ with $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ and determined by density functional theory (DFT) calculations.32 The ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ cation is octahedrally coordinated by two trans-oriented 12, two trans-oriented bromide anions, MeOH, and DMF. The coordinated distances between cobalt and ligand 12, bromide anions, MeOH and DMF were 1.98, 2.52, 2.25 and $2.09\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , respectively. This gives a reasonable assembled structure in 2D space with the cavity size.
Next the sensing ability of the $\mathbf{CoBr}_{2}$ coordination polymer gel 12 was studied as a selective chemosensor for toxic gases containing chloride such as HCl, $\mathbf{\{}s o c l}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{COCl})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{cocl}_{2}$ (phosgene) using UV-vis spectroscopy. The UV-vis spectrum of $\mathbf{CoBr}_{2}$ coordination polymer gel 12 exhibits an absorption band at $475~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ with a red color, suggesting the formation of octahedral $(O_{\mathrm{h}})$ complexes.32 Introduction of a small amount of $\mathbf{COCl}_{2}$ (phosgene) gas causes a red shift of $195~\mathrm{nm}$ , resulting in a new band at $670\mathrm{nm}$ , and the expected blue color. It is likely that the bromide anions were replaced with chloride because the phosgene may decompose in the gel state,32 releasing chloride anions. An absorption band at $670\mathrm{nm}$ is characteristic of a tetrahedral ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ complex $\left(T_{\mathrm{d}},\ ^{4}A_{2},\ ^{4}T_{1}(P)\right)$ . In addition, the fluorescence intensity of the coordination polymer gel 12 gradually decreases upon exposure to phosgene gas. Introduction of HCl, $\operatorname{socl}_{2}$ and $(\mathrm{COCl})_{2}$ gases into the $\mathbf{CoBr}_{2}$ coordination polymer gel 12 each induced color shifts from red to blue. These results indicate ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ in a CPG with 12 changes from an octahedral to a tetrahedral coordination upon introduction of a chloride ion. A portable chemosensor was prepared for the detection of the gases containing chloride atoms. The red color of a disk-type pellet of dried gel of 12 changed to blue within $10\mathrm{~s~}$ when exposed to phosgene gas (Fig. 13). The color change was found to be fully reversible when a disk-type pellet prepared from the gelled 12 is exposed to phosgene gas and subsequently rinsed with a DMF/MeOH solution containing HBr. By measuring the changes in the intensities of the absorption and fluorescence emission of a CPG of 12 with chloride, the absorption and fluorescence changes for coordination polymer gel of 12 were found to correspond to the range 1–20 ppb of phosgene. This is the first example of a CPG as a chemosensor for gases containing chloride.
## 8. Functionalized graphene-based CP gels as TNT sensor
Graphene and its functionalized derivatives are versatile building blocks for carbon-based materials, owing to their unique two- or three-dimensional (2D or 3D) structures and excellent physical and chemical properties.33 Recent work has demonstrated that self-assembly is a powerful technique for constructing hierarchical graphene-based nanomaterials with novel functions.34 In particular, the self-assembly of nano-sized graphene into macroscopic materials can translate the properties of individual graphene sheets into the resulting macrostructures which showed numerous breakthrough applications in optoelectronics,35 energystorage,36 biomedicine and chemosensing.37
A prime example of this was reported by Jung et al.38 with the formation of a metal–organic framework/azobenzoic acidfunctionalized graphene oxide composite hydrogel (MOF/A-GO) in the presence of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and its application as a chemosensor for the detection of trinitrotoluene (TNT) molecules.38 The preparation of azobenzoic acid-functionalized graphene oxide (abbreviated as A-GO) is shown in Fig. 14. Initially, epoxy and hydroxyl groups were removed by reduction with $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . Next, the chemically reduced GO (r-GO) was functionalized with azobenzoic acid (Fig. 14) using the diazonium grafting method, allowing the basal planes to become extended by azobenzoic acid groups. The red-shifted $\pi{-}\pi^{\ast}$ absorption band of r-GO at $260~\mathrm{{nm}}$ compared to the band of GO at $241~\mathrm{{nm}}$ is consistent with the partial recovery of the conjugated network. Additional absorption appeared at $400~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , this wavelength signifying the azobenzoic acid attached onto r-GO.
When an A-GO $\left(10\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}\right).$ suspension containing $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and ligand 14 was mixed under ultrasonication for a few seconds, the metal organic framework/azobenzoic acid-functionalized graphene oxide composite hydrogel (abbreviated as MOF/ A-GO) was obtained (Fig. 15). On the other hand, ligand 14 and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ without the A-GO did not form a hydrogel at the same concentrations. These findings suggest that A-GO acted as an interlayer to form the stable MOF structure in the gel phase. The MOF/A-GO has a well-defined nanorod structure with a width of $100{-}150\ \mathrm{~nm}$ and a length of several micrometers. The sheet structure of A-GO was not obtained in the MOF/A-GO. The results support the view that A-GO is bound to ligand 14 by a coordination bond with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ , forming the MOF structure.
The potential for using this system as a chemosensor was studied by exposing MOF/A-GO films to trinitrotoluene (TNT) and dinitrotoluene (DNT) vapours. Films of MOF/A-GO were drop casted from water, and the films showed $70\%$ fluorescence quenching after one minute of exposure to saturated
TNT vapours. Nearly $98\%$ quenching was observed upon continuous exposure for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . As expected, the MOF/A-GO film exhibited a gradual decrease in the fluorescent intensity upon the addition of TNT, which is attributed to charge-transfer interactions occurring between the electron-deficient aromatic ring of TNT and the electron-rich aromatic group of 14 in MOF/ A-GO. The use of MOF/A-GO for the detection of DNT vapours was also studied. A film of MOF/A-GO showed a quenching effect on fluorescence when exposed to saturated DNT vapours under the same conditions; however, the quenching efficiency was found to be much less as compared to that of TNT vapours. After exposure for $10~\mathrm{min}$ , only $2\%$ quenching was observed in the presence of DNT compared to $98\%$ quenching for the case of TNT.
## 9. Pyridine-based CP gels as a drug delivery system
Molecular gels have emerged as a logical alternative to current polymeric gels for controlled drug-delivery. Several hydrogels of low molecular weight amphiphiles have been widely employed as intelligent carriers in controlled drug-delivery systems.39 The self-assembly of the hydrogelators plays a key role during the process of gelation in a supramolecular hydrogel. Therefore, triggering or regulating the self-assembly of hydrogelators becomes an essential step in controlling the states and properties of supramoelcular hydrogels, which is normally achieved by chemical or physical perturbations (e.g., pH, temperature, ionic strength and ultrasonic agitation). Self-assembled molecular gels as a drug delivery system have been successfully demonstrated for regenerative medicine by several groups.39,40
Very recently, Jung et al.41 reported a pyridine-based ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ coordination polymer hydrogel as a drug delivery system. The hydrogel 15 is pH responsive and precipitated upon adjustment to acidic and basic pH values. Furthermore, upon adjusting the $\mathsf{p H}$ from acidic to neutral conditions, the formation of a complex allows the recovery of the gel structure. Compound 15 could also be gelated with a variety of copper anions such as ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}$ , ${\mathbf{NO_{3}}}^{-}$ and $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ . The $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ coordination polymeric gel 15 clearly displayed a fibrillar network. The coordination polymeric structure of 15 with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ was demonstrated by density functional theory (DFT) calculations. The optimized structure of the complex showed two water molecules and four ligands coordinated to each ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ion with a possibly octahedral geometry with the two water molecules in a trans position. It was also determined that two ligands bridge two ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions and are thus coordinated to both simultaneously. The distance between the two copper(II) ions was calculated to be $16.25\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and the distance between the two nitrogen atoms of the pyridine across the cavity was calculated to be $8.24\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ .
Jung et al.41 also demonstrated pH-triggered delivery using a ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ coordination polymer hydrogel. The curcumin molecules were first stably encapsulated in the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ coordination polymeric gel 15 (Fig. 16). Encapsulation is thought to occur by hydrophobic or hydrogen-bond interactions in hydrophobic cavity between ligand and curcumin molecules. $1.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of an aqueous solution $\mathrm{(pH}=5\mathrm{)}$ was added to the preformed gel which had been kept at $37~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for $80~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Initially, the added solution was colorless (Fig. 16A; c). After $80\mathrm{min}$ , visual changes occurred (Fig. 16A; d), i.e., gel 15 became soluble, and the top solution became yellow in color which indicated that encapsulated curcumin had been released into the solution at $\mathrm{\pH}=5$ . Interestingly, initial aliquots did not show any absorbance peak (Fig. 16B; a), but aliquots collected after $80~\mathrm{\min}$ showed absorption maxima at $425~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ (Fig. 16B; b), which corresponded to the absorption peak of curcumin (Fig. 16B; c). When the $\mathsf{p H}$ was adjusted to $\sim5$ , approximately $100\%$ of curcumin was efficiently released into the aqueous phase over $80~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The results strongly indicated that the curcumin that was encapsulated in the hydrogel was effectively released by pH control.
## 10. Rational ligand tuning to create CPG nanoparticle templates
The ability to tailor self-assembly to create specific nanostructure templates is one of the factors that have inspired researchers to use CPGs as a stepping stone to other functional nanomaterials. Slightly changing the structure of the organic bridging ligand is a simple way to impart large morphological changes in the self-assembled nanostructures. The inclusion of a new double bond, aromatic ring, or chelating heteroatom changes the nature of the gel. Slight changes in the ligand can afford gel structures with widely varying properties imparting the functionality of nanomaterial templates with tunable nanotube diameters.
Steed et al.42 have designed several interesting ligands by varying the inner section of the ligand (16–28) and while keeping the metal coordinating aminopyridine ends. Tuning ligand structure allows for gels with varying morphologies and physical properties. The ligands were self-assembled in the presence of both ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions. The morphologies were observed by SEM and further characterized by rheological studies to fully understand the stability of the gels against shear force.
The addition of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ metal ions significantly enhanced the gelation ability of pyridyl ureas, known to be reluctant to form gels as free ligands (enhanced by the hydrogen bonding, crystal formation). However, addition of metal ions leads to a metal cross-linked urea tape motif, or a metal cross-linked structure to provide a 3D network capable of encapsulating solvent inside the channels of the fibrous structure. Adding metals to a certain class of ligands may find applications as thickening agents in targeted applications, as demonstrated by rheological studies.
Steed et al.43 demonstrated the synthesis of CPGs in a series of publications using pyridine coupled (diaminopyridine) bisurea products 29 in comparison to 30 (diamino benzene) analogous. The 29-based building blocks conveniently produced 3-D assemblies through coordination induced cross-linking (Fig. 17). However, 30 assembles lacking the three dimensional structure and entanglements, which results in very weak gel structures on assembly. The bisurea derivatives formed polymeric structures by complexing with silver ions to afford CPGs. This polymer eventually reduces the coordinated ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ into metallic $\mathbf{Ag}(0)$ through a slow reduction process enabled by the urea moiety. Interestingly, this group was able to increase the rate of formation of these nanoparticles by illuminating the gel with UV radiation $(365~\mathrm{nm})$ ).
The present study demonstrated the creation of hybrid materials through Ag mediated coordination and further reduction of the metal ions to metallic particles. Steed et al. also studied the effect of nanoparticle incorporation of NPs into the gel matrix, which showed an increase in the stability of the gel and was thoroughly monitored by rheological studies.
Jung et al.44 have reported the preparation of silver nanoparticles by using a coordination polymer gel of the tetrazolebased ligand (31) containing ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ ions and its catalytic capabilities for templating. It was found that the CPGs with high concentrations of the silver salt, including samples containing 3 and 4 equivalents of $\mathrm{\bfAgClO_{4}}$ , showed large nanoparticles accompanied by a size distribution ranging from 1.5 to $3.5\quad\mathrm{nm}$ and at the highest concentration even up to $2.0\mathrm{-}5.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Choosing to investigate the reduction of 4-nitrophenol (4-NP) by sodium borohydride $\left(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\right)$ as a model reaction, the reduction can be monitored by the disappearance of the $400~\mathrm{{nm}}$ peak with the concomitant appearance of a new peak at $300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ after the catalysis. This peak was then attributed to 4-aminophenol (4-AP). It was finally confirmed that the silver nanoparticles deposited onto the gel fibers catalyzed the reduction reaction.
## 11. CPG mediated crystal growth
The pharmaceutical industry pays specific attention to the presence of polymorphic crystal systems when designing novel drugs. Being able to control crystal growth is of the utmost importance as the in vivo dissolution rates of different crystal polymorphs may vary. Steed et al. have developed the pyridine bisurea ligand 32 capable of gelling methanol solutions of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ halides (Fig. 18).45 The crystal growth of the mood-stabilizing anticonvulsant carbamazepine was monitored and was found to be dependent on the concentration of copper halide added during the formation of the gel. Daughter crystals nucleate from gel-encased parent needles, which have restricted crystal surface area leaving specific areas available for the promotion of crystal growth.
CPGs can serve as matrices to grow crystals in an inert environment allowing for slow crystal growth to afford large uniform crystals. In certain cases, $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ gels of 32 were found to promote crystallization where a corresponding solution was not. Drug/ligand co-crystals were not observed, indicating the effective phase-separation of these two species during the gelation/crystallization process.
## 12. Conclusion
The inclusion of metal ions into low molecular weight polymer gel systems has proven a great asset in combining the functionality of traditional coordionation chemistry with the wide array of applications for gels. Advances made in the past decade involving the arrangement of coordinated polymer gel systems have been seen to possess great utility in the same areas and with great results in between organic and inorganic chemistry. Recent attempts to delineate the differently organized systems have been increasingly successful as X-ray diffraction and electron microscopy provide more information about internal fibril and 3D structure, distinguishing these systems as CPGs, MOFs and metallogels. As techniques to study coordinated polymer systems continue to develop they look to tackle the issue of studying wet gels with X-ray diffraction and electron microscopy, which often require solid crystals, dry powders, or xerogels.
Of the various CP gels reviewed here the catalytic systems utilize metal ions for catalysis, and the ligands for their imparted structure. Additionally, if a CPG responds to stimuli, either after exposure to light or the inclusion of a foreign species into the gel network, the systems are often transformed resulting in changes in the metal–ligand coordination. This often results in changes in the nature of the trapped solvent, or the liquid mixture. Be it a pH change, or the inclusion of organic or ionic species into a hydrogel, the bridging ligand’s functional groups and the metal–ligand bonds are often manipulated by shifting absorbance ranges or inherent fluorescent properties indicating that the solvent environment plays a large role in the nature of the CP gel. These systems may be well employed in environmental fields, responding to changes in a gel’s surroundings, furthermore allowing for simple deconstruction of the fibril network through heating or sonication to afford monomers and mixtures for cleaning or reuse. Catalytic activity, chemosensing, pH responsivity, and fluorescence are just the beginning for CPGs, as new arrangements of metal ions and bridging ligands are discovered and rationally designed our understanding of these systems will only improve. Work on chiral systems, in addition to preliminary drug delivery studies, shows that there is a strong interest in applying non-toxic systems to biological fields, similar to work being done with traditional low molecular-weight gelators. The utility and possibility is as limitless as the number of metal ions and organic ligands that may be paired, and this is just another reason that CP gels will reside at the forefront of chemical technology in the future.
